WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.398
So here's some fairly unconventional advice.

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:07.798
I think that if you are really struggling with a course or failing it then either that

00:00:08.019 --> 00:00:13.649
subject matter really isn't for you or you need to get better friends. And often

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.840
I think that last part getting better friends is pretty much what it comes down to.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:22.920
The advice that I'm going to talk about mainly applies for

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:29.879
university level subjects and my experience in studying at University and how to get good grades there

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.029
But I think this probably applies at school as well

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.710
I think it's almost kind of obvious at that level that if you're friends with people that

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:41.699
are sort of leading you astray things aren't going to work out well for you.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:47.789
But I'm more talking about people that are actually, you know, really keen to study really came to get good grades

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:52.890
But for some reason they just don't and this phrase "if you get bad grades

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.760
you need better friends" isn't actually something I came up with myself.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.199
It's something that I heard a professor telling to the students in his class.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:06.779
So at that level, I guess the professor wanted the students to learn and do well

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:12.449
but he was aware enough that I guess there was a lot of material to learn and that

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.939
For student trying to learn at all on their own

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:18.239
they're just not going to do as well as someone who's

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.590
going about it a bit smarter and is working with other people. And

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:26.459
When I say working with other people, of course, I'm not talking about

00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:32.039
copying off people or like not learning the content for yourself, but doing it smartly

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.659
working in teams to figure out problems that alone would have

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.769
taken you way too long to figure out like you can actually use other people

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.970
To your advantage in a way that benefits all of you.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:53.610
Of course again, there are situations when you can't work with other people at universities. So on take-home tests or exams or

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:58.589
assessed work. But for assignments and studying and all that sort of stuff

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:02.070
it's kind of expected that you would work with someone.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:08.099
It's it's not expected that someone's going to be able to learn all the course content and do all the assignments like purely on their

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:12.479
own. I don't think that's a smart thing to do at all. So the way I see it is that

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:20.909
actually, if you're studying at University, then the most important skill that's going to help you get good grades is being able to socialise and

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:27.240
being able to make friends with strangers and and communicate and work in a team. Now

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:28.810
I know that

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:34.560
for like a lot of you guys study maths or physics that subject like that because that's what I talk about on my channel and

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.230
Often socializing isn't our most favorite activity. In fact, I in person

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.230
I'm an incredibly shy and socially awkward person.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:48.599
So if someone gave me this advice ages ago, it would probably horrify me and I would say no.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.170
Learning physics shouldn't be about how good you are at socializing

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.539
it should be all about your inherent

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.329
mathematical skills and you should be able to be like that lone genius and and you would think like

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.000
Einstein never had to work in a team

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.430
He just came up with all these theories on his own. But you know like I've been through the Uni system

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:14.549
And I've sort of seen how this works and I've come to the realization that there is no such thing as the lone genius.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.430
But that's sort of a whole nother topic

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:24.809
Essentially the way that a lot of Unis work in the way at least mine definitely did was

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:32.970
to get the most out of the course and to get the most out of what the lecturer is offering you and and all the

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:39.089
Opportunities available to you, you really need to work with other people and if you don't have friends in a class

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:43.080
It's going to be really hard for you no matter how smart you think you are.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:49.680
So my biggest study tip is to find some friends early in your course and

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.789
you know, if you don't know how to find friends, here's some ideas like

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:55.870
the

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:57.870
most valuable friends

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.610
I guess I made at university were the people that I met in like the front row of lectures

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:07.079
it seems to be then the people that sit in the front row are like really keen and

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:10.109
often really friendly really open to like

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:17.249
I don't know working together and and having chats about the content and I think in the front row of lectures you can find some

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:18.510
really valuable people.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:24.119
I think uni and finding friends is always harder for people that don't live on campus and don't have that sort of

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:30.540
automatic friend group that comes along with living with other people, but I think it really is possible to meet friends and lectures

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:35.879
It can be as easy as just like asking them what their major is and then asking them

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:41.100
You know about some assignment you have guys have coming up whether they like to work together and the more

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:47.309
Momentum you can build around sort of a study group and add more people into it and really build that

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:51.059
family and that sense of belonging and and

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.219
Comradeship around your course, it's gonna be a lot better for you.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.029
So let me give you some examples from my life and what I'm talking about here.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:07.800
Now today is actually my 23rd birthday and it's the anniversary of a time back in first year when I spent my birthday

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:14.099
having a real miserable time in a mathematical modelling lab. It was an 8 a.m

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:19.830
Lab on a Friday and I mean that timing is awful about why this was a really awful day for me

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.499
was because I just had no friends in this class.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:29.850
I guess the content wasn't even that difficult. I remember this particular lab. We were on MATLAB and we were like trying to

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.700
code in some functions to

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:36.270
generate trig functions like the sine function

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.090
through a Taylor expansion.

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:44.399
We were coding it and I feel like this would kind of be really easy for me to do now

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.570
It's not even hard in concept, but at the time I just had like no idea what I was doing.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.160
I didn't know how to use MATLAB

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.160
I didn't know anything about Taylor expansion

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:56.519
but I also just had no friends in this class and it was the only class like this because

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:00.090
It was the only class I was taking different to like my main

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:08.039
group of solid friends who I'd made in my other courses and I was just like sitting in this room and

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:14.189
everyone felt really unapproachable. I didn't know anyone and I just felt like I had to struggle through it alone.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:20.190
And I was like, that's fine. I can just like read up, you know explanations for this and try and figure it out myself, but

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:25.679
Honestly, it was just miserable. I took so long to try and figure it out

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:31.290
and if I just had someone beside me who I felt comfortable like talking to and being like oh

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.529
Are you up to this step? How do you think we go from here?

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:39.130
It just would have been so much better and it was actually after I had such a bad time in that course.

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:41.290
But I was like, I'm never gonna let that happen again

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:45.670
I'm always going to make sure you know in the first week of a new course

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:50.319
My priority is going to be making sure I have a friend in this class like that

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:55.929
I know someone and that I can I can always ask someone for help and I think that was really so important for me was

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:58.010
prioritizing

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:01.390
not letting myself be alone and struggling alone in a course.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:09.039
The way that I would work with my friends who I made in classes that maybe we'd like sit together in lectures and

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:13.240
maybe joke around a little bit or like talk about around them stuff

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.809
But then like when it came to doing assignments often

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:21.730
There would be like facebook messenger chats or groups or something and that would be like, you know

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.390
How do you do this

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:24.470
certain type of

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:30.910
question? And then people would sort of explain it to you. And and it really worked both ways because if you were struggling with something

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.570
You could ask

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:36.429
Your friends for help and they would often, you know, get you out of the rut

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.739
You're in much faster than you could get out yourself.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:43.150
But also if you are the person who already understands what's going on

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:47.589
and you'd probably could just do this particular assignment all by yourself no worries

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:54.429
I think it's really valuable for you to help other people out and to not be a jerk but to

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:56.480
You know, really

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.940
Bring people along with you.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:05.650
And I think it really benefits you because you are getting practice explaining something and you're actually making sure you understand it better yourself

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:11.859
You're solidifying things and you've got these people who sort of like are really grateful for your help.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:13.960
So I really think it goes both ways.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:19.210
If you're struggling in class make it your priority to make good friends

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:24.729
and people that you know are aligned with what you're trying to achieve in the class and

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.660
Aren't going to drag you down and are really going to help you get there

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.089
And even if you are doing really well

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:35.139
Maybe go out of your way a little bit to help others to still

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:41.979
appreciate the value of friends when you're studying and to know that it also values you to explain things to other people.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:44.620
Thanks for watching.

